WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.104 --> 00:00:07.790
ROBERT KUBIS: Hello, and
welcome to this Next OnAir

00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:10.280
talk about how to choose
the right database

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:12.290
for your workloads.

00:00:12.290 --> 00:00:13.250
I'm Robert Kubis.

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:15.140
I'm a Google Cloud
developer advocate,

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:17.480
and I've worked with
storage and databases

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:20.030
for the majority of my
time here at Google.

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:25.140
You can find me on
Twitter with @hostirosti.

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:29.280
Now, if you look at application
and database migration

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:34.050
journeys, we do understand that
these are dynamic and diverse.

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:36.630
So if you are coming from
an on-premises system

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:39.570
and moving into the cloud, if
you're moving from one cloud

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:43.920
to another, or if you're looking
at a multi-cloud strategy,

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:47.400
even if you are in the business
of developing something

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:51.390
new from scratch or
extending your applications

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:53.610
with new applications
from scratch,

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:56.790
we want to meet you where you
are and want to support you

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:58.390
in those journeys.

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:03.340
So today I want to focus on
three major journeys here.

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:06.910
First one is migration, so
like for like migration,

00:01:06.910 --> 00:01:09.330
lift and shift,
lift and optimize.

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:11.550
Here, you're coming
from a system

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:15.000
that you're familiar with and
you would like to stay with,

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.810
but you want to move into
the cloud, reducing your TCO,

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:23.340
for instance, or having actually
the possibility for faster

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:26.610
migrations, fewer
applications, rewrites,

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:31.410
and enable the modernization
once you are on the cloud.

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:34.560
Second, I want to talk about
modernization journeys.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.600
Here you are looking
at challenges

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:39.660
that you have with your
existing databases where

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:42.630
you want to move off
your existing databases

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:45.660
and want to move on a cloud
native database, for instance,

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:48.660
or one of our managed
open source solutions.

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:52.170
This can help you reduce
and eliminate reliance

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:53.280
on a single database.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:55.080
It allows you to
rethink how you're

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:57.180
using your databases,
for instance,

00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:59.940
consolidating many
databases into one

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:03.030
or changing the database
that you're using.

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:06.600
And third, I want to touch on
innovation and transformation.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:10.650
Here, I want to focus on what
are the databases that we

00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:13.320
are offering as fully
managed solutions

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:16.470
to remove the toil
from your plate

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:20.310
so that you can concentrate on
solving and building the things

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:21.930
that your customers need.

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:24.750
Here, you have the
ability to really tap

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:29.580
into systems that are scalable,
available, SLA-backed, and that

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:33.640
help you to advance
your business.

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:36.410
So let's start with migrate.

00:02:36.410 --> 00:02:38.630
So the migration journey,
to be successful,

00:02:38.630 --> 00:02:40.610
is a multi-step process.

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:43.070
And I want to touch on this
because this is important

00:02:43.070 --> 00:02:46.430
if you want to have a
successful database migration.

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:48.540
You start with
assessment and discovery.

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:52.010
You want to see where you
are, where you're coming from,

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:56.090
and what are the criterias along
the way that build success.

00:02:56.090 --> 00:02:57.980
Make these criterias
in a way that you

00:02:57.980 --> 00:03:01.330
can check on your
journey your success

00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:03.440
criterias along the way.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:05.700
Then you want to set
up this environment.

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:07.680
So there are different
ways of doing this.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:10.490
You can basically start an
organization, Google Cloud,

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:13.290
create your projects,
set up network peering,

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:16.580
set up private networks to
always have your data secure

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:17.870
when you're moving it.

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:20.540
Or if you have a lot of
data, you can use something

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:23.040
like Transfer Appliance
that we are offering,

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:25.180
which we are shipping
to your data center,

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:27.890
where you can load data
onto it, ship it back,

00:03:27.890 --> 00:03:30.020
and then it gets
loaded into GCS,

00:03:30.020 --> 00:03:33.830
where you can load it back into
the database of your choice.

00:03:33.830 --> 00:03:35.990
Then you have the data
migration, of course.

00:03:35.990 --> 00:03:38.870
Check with monitoring if
this migration is going

00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:41.960
well, validate it,
and then have always

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:46.200
a strategy of if something
goes wrong how to roll back.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:48.380
Especially if you
do online migration,

00:03:48.380 --> 00:03:50.120
you want to have
rollback strategies,

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:52.580
and you want to
have them tested.

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:54.070
So what are the options?

00:03:54.070 --> 00:03:56.440
You see here on
the left that there

00:03:56.440 --> 00:03:58.910
are a lot of databases
that are around.

00:03:58.910 --> 00:04:00.880
There are open source databases.

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:02.820
There are third-party databases.

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:07.390
There are relational databases,
key-value store, column store,

00:04:07.390 --> 00:04:11.750
caches, really streaming
databases, a lot of databases

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:12.820
that are around.

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:14.320
Now, if you look
at those databases,

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:16.740
where can you end
up on Google Cloud?

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:19.630
So you see on the right,
we have infrastructure

00:04:19.630 --> 00:04:22.029
as a service or
managed databases

00:04:22.029 --> 00:04:24.670
that you can choose to end up.

00:04:24.670 --> 00:04:27.190
Especially for
homogeneous migration,

00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:29.920
you want to look if your
databases are supported

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:32.170
by our fully managed services.

00:04:32.170 --> 00:04:36.880
Or you can also get help
from our managed partners

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:39.770
to go into those solutions.

00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:41.030
So let's resume.

00:04:41.030 --> 00:04:43.120
For instance, if you
have all those databases

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:45.580
on the left side, you can
always go with the way

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:49.450
and self-manage them on Compute
Engine or Kubernetes Engine,

00:04:49.450 --> 00:04:50.440
if you like.

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:55.180
Now, if you are using, for
instance, MySQL, PL/SQL, SQL

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:58.330
Server, you can use
one of our managed

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:00.550
solutions with Cloud SQL.

00:05:00.550 --> 00:05:04.210
Now, for each base, for instance
if you have HBase workloads,

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:06.790
our Cloud Bigtable managed
solution and cloud native

00:05:06.790 --> 00:05:09.250
database has an HBase API.

00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:13.170
And so you can end up
with your HBase workloads

00:05:13.170 --> 00:05:15.380
in Cloud Bigtables directly.

00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:18.520
Now, if you're looking
into cache solutions,

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:20.290
you can use Cloud Memorystore.

00:05:20.290 --> 00:05:22.360
Or for writers, for
instance, we also

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:26.260
have managed partner solutions
with our open source partners

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:28.300
to provide you a
fully managed solution

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:30.610
that is integrated
with billing, that

00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:33.340
is integrated with monitoring,
and easily accessible

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:35.410
through the marketplace.

00:05:35.410 --> 00:05:38.800
And then, last but not least, we
also have Bare Metal Solution.

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:41.950
So Bare Metal Solution I will
go a little bit more in detail

00:05:41.950 --> 00:05:44.130
later, but that also
allows you to bring

00:05:44.130 --> 00:05:47.260
your third-party database
workloads closer to our data

00:05:47.260 --> 00:05:49.160
centers.

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:51.600
So I want to focus
a bit on SQL, the

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:54.670
how do we support
you in SQL migration.

00:05:54.670 --> 00:05:57.440
So one of the things that, for
instance, with MySQL support

00:05:57.440 --> 00:05:59.580
is external replication.

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:01.880
So you can set up
external replication

00:06:01.880 --> 00:06:05.270
of your existing MySQL
databases on-premises

00:06:05.270 --> 00:06:09.770
or on another cloud, and this
way, catch up with your data

00:06:09.770 --> 00:06:13.700
in our cloud on Cloud
SQL and then switch over.

00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:17.480
We have also a lot of
other functionality that

00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:20.840
helps you to move your
data and your applications

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:22.410
into the cloud.

00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:24.290
Now, I want to point
out here that we

00:06:24.290 --> 00:06:27.040
are partnering with a lot
of migration partners.

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:30.960
You see a screenshot of logos
down there in the slide.

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:33.770
And if you basically
want to have help,

00:06:33.770 --> 00:06:37.640
if you want to have experienced
partners, experienced partners

00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:39.800
in your migration
journey, please

00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:42.350
feel free to reach out to
those migration partners that

00:06:42.350 --> 00:06:47.358
have done [INAUDIBLE] migration
successfully onto Cloud SQL.

00:06:47.358 --> 00:06:48.900
And last but not
least, I also wanted

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:53.550
to point out that you can
get more information on what

00:06:53.550 --> 00:06:56.640
features we have, for instance,
with Cloud SQL coming,

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:01.530
and how to migrate or recover
with Cloud SQL in the talks

00:07:01.530 --> 00:07:03.810
that you see in the
Learn More section.

00:07:03.810 --> 00:07:05.900
Now, what is Bare
Metal Solution?

00:07:05.900 --> 00:07:07.440
With Bare Metal
Solution, we allow

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:11.940
you to migrate or move
your Oracle workflows

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:13.620
closer to Google Cloud.

00:07:13.620 --> 00:07:16.860
So maybe you can't move
off Oracle right now,

00:07:16.860 --> 00:07:19.530
or you want to move
at a later point,

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:22.080
and you want to move your
central workloads closer

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:23.430
to Google Cloud.

00:07:23.430 --> 00:07:24.930
Now, with Bare
Metal Solution, you

00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:28.980
have some solution that is
possible in the sense of that

00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:30.840
we have purpose-built
hardware that

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:33.660
is certified that brings
your workloads closer

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:34.590
to Google Cloud.

00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:36.300
We have integrated billing.

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:41.850
This is a very low latency
setup with our data centers.

00:07:41.850 --> 00:07:46.140
And we allow you to bring
your own DBAs or your service

00:07:46.140 --> 00:07:49.830
integration partners that
you prefer with the solution.

00:07:49.830 --> 00:07:52.440
Now, if you want to learn more
about the Bare Metal Solutions,

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:57.230
please check out
the talk DBS213.

00:07:57.230 --> 00:07:57.790
All right.

00:07:57.790 --> 00:08:00.490
With this, I want to go
into the modernization

00:08:00.490 --> 00:08:02.480
section of this talk.

00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:05.260
So again, I want
to touch on-- also,

00:08:05.260 --> 00:08:08.470
modernization
journey is something

00:08:08.470 --> 00:08:11.140
that you need to look
at the multiple steps

00:08:11.140 --> 00:08:13.510
that you are [? trotting ?]
on during this journey.

00:08:13.510 --> 00:08:15.190
Now, this is a little
bit more complex.

00:08:15.190 --> 00:08:17.860
You see here, I
didn't do justice

00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:19.990
with just adding this app
into my creation step,

00:08:19.990 --> 00:08:22.000
but each of these
steps is touched if you

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:23.650
do a modernization journey.

00:08:23.650 --> 00:08:25.690
Now in a modernization
journey, you

00:08:25.690 --> 00:08:28.050
have things you have
to think about, OK, I

00:08:28.050 --> 00:08:30.700
might have to do a data
model transformation.

00:08:30.700 --> 00:08:32.440
I have to do my
data transformation

00:08:32.440 --> 00:08:34.120
if I move into the cloud.

00:08:34.120 --> 00:08:36.100
There are maybe
different things that you

00:08:36.100 --> 00:08:40.120
need to change in your apps to
make that actually possible.

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:44.230
But again, all this should
be starting at an assessment.

00:08:44.230 --> 00:08:45.420
Do POCs.

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:47.050
Set up the success criteria.

00:08:47.050 --> 00:08:49.810
Set up the environment,
and have strategies

00:08:49.810 --> 00:08:52.070
if something goes wrong.

00:08:52.070 --> 00:08:56.000
Now, there are three main
cloud native databases

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:59.930
that I want to touch on here
for your migration journeys.

00:08:59.930 --> 00:09:01.540
Number one is Firestore.

00:09:01.540 --> 00:09:03.820
So Firestore is our
fast, fully managed,

00:09:03.820 --> 00:09:06.590
serverless NoSQL cloud database.

00:09:06.590 --> 00:09:10.910
So it allows you to especially
build really powerful VAP

00:09:10.910 --> 00:09:12.750
and mobile applications.

00:09:12.750 --> 00:09:14.360
It is a document-based database.

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:19.000
And it allows you also to sync
your devices in real time.

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:21.070
Now, Bigtable is a
fast and fully managed

00:09:21.070 --> 00:09:25.060
wide-column database or
key-value NoSQL database that

00:09:25.060 --> 00:09:27.820
allows you low
latency, scalability,

00:09:27.820 --> 00:09:30.740
and reliability for
massive workloads

00:09:30.740 --> 00:09:36.500
where you need fast lookups,
fast writes, and fast scans.

00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:38.740
Now, Cloud Spanner
is our database

00:09:38.740 --> 00:09:42.940
that addresses the [INAUDIBLE]
and relational database market.

00:09:42.940 --> 00:09:45.610
With Cloud Spanner, you
have an enterprise-grade,

00:09:45.610 --> 00:09:49.350
globally-distributed, and
strongly consistent SQL

00:09:49.350 --> 00:09:53.320
database that has
asset functionalities

00:09:53.320 --> 00:09:57.670
and that you have basically
built-in disaster recovery

00:09:57.670 --> 00:10:02.560
and high availability
with horizontal scale.

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:04.870
Now, what do we see
in our customers?

00:10:04.870 --> 00:10:07.480
What are the journeys that
we see a lot of times?

00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:09.480
So here, I want to point
out that, for instance,

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:12.650
for the [INAUDIBLE] and
SQL database modernization

00:10:12.650 --> 00:10:15.750
on workloads, we see
a lot of customers

00:10:15.750 --> 00:10:19.140
that hit certain challenges
with their existing

00:10:19.140 --> 00:10:20.870
relational databases.

00:10:20.870 --> 00:10:23.550
And they want to move
into a more managed,

00:10:23.550 --> 00:10:27.420
scalable, and
SLA-backed solution.

00:10:27.420 --> 00:10:29.820
So many of those
customers are looking

00:10:29.820 --> 00:10:32.160
at Cloud Spanner
at the destination

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:35.290
from a relational database.

00:10:35.290 --> 00:10:38.760
Now here, challenges are
addressed with scalability.

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:41.040
You have an SLA
up to five nines.

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:42.960
You have built-in
high availability

00:10:42.960 --> 00:10:44.230
and disaster recovery.

00:10:44.230 --> 00:10:46.830
So there are many things that
help you basically making

00:10:46.830 --> 00:10:48.288
this decision.

00:10:48.288 --> 00:10:49.830
Now, other workloads
that you can see

00:10:49.830 --> 00:10:52.380
is, for instance,
Cassandra workloads.

00:10:52.380 --> 00:10:55.260
Now, there's a little
bit of a differentiation

00:10:55.260 --> 00:10:56.760
here that you have to look at.

00:10:56.760 --> 00:10:59.580
So for Cassandra workload,
it's really important is,

00:10:59.580 --> 00:11:02.530
why did you move to
Cassandra in the first place?

00:11:02.530 --> 00:11:06.420
Did you try to address
a challenge like

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:09.910
you had scalability issues,
and you were saying,

00:11:09.910 --> 00:11:14.640
OK, I wanted to address the
scalability issue challenge,

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:16.710
and that's why I
moved to Cassandra,

00:11:16.710 --> 00:11:19.470
which is a horizontal,
scalable database system.

00:11:19.470 --> 00:11:24.510
But by that, you sacrificed some
of the consistencies, qualities

00:11:24.510 --> 00:11:27.130
that you have with a
relational database.

00:11:27.130 --> 00:11:33.300
Now, if you weren't happy with
sacrificing these constraints,

00:11:33.300 --> 00:11:35.520
the relational
constraints that you have,

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:39.090
the consistency constraints
that you have in your workloads,

00:11:39.090 --> 00:11:42.180
and you had to build this
into your application, which

00:11:42.180 --> 00:11:46.470
is error prone, and difficult,
and quite an overhead,

00:11:46.470 --> 00:11:48.630
then many of those
customers are actually

00:11:48.630 --> 00:11:51.420
moving to Cloud Spanner
because you get back

00:11:51.420 --> 00:11:54.620
here the consistency, but you
keep the horizontal scalability

00:11:54.620 --> 00:11:56.440
before your workloads.

00:11:56.440 --> 00:11:58.260
Now, if you moved
to Cassandra because

00:11:58.260 --> 00:12:01.590
of the horizontal
scalability, and you're

00:12:01.590 --> 00:12:06.210
fine sacrificing the
consistency in here,

00:12:06.210 --> 00:12:09.420
then we see customers moving
to Cloud Bigtable, where

00:12:09.420 --> 00:12:12.210
you have a level of consistency,
but [? eventually ?]

00:12:12.210 --> 00:12:17.890
consistency for replication
across zones and regions.

00:12:17.890 --> 00:12:21.570
Now, if you, for instance,
come from a document store,

00:12:21.570 --> 00:12:24.060
we see quite a lot
of customers that

00:12:24.060 --> 00:12:25.910
are looking, for
instance, moving

00:12:25.910 --> 00:12:30.820
to Firestore as a destination
from mongoDB, for instance.

00:12:30.820 --> 00:12:31.330
All right.

00:12:31.330 --> 00:12:34.210
So what how do we support you
actually in these database

00:12:34.210 --> 00:12:35.120
modernization?

00:12:35.120 --> 00:12:37.270
So there are quite
a couple of things

00:12:37.270 --> 00:12:41.650
that you can look at for this
support in the modernization.

00:12:41.650 --> 00:12:44.170
On the one hand, we are
building more and more tools

00:12:44.170 --> 00:12:46.150
that help you migrate.

00:12:46.150 --> 00:12:49.240
So we are integrating
with industry standards

00:12:49.240 --> 00:12:51.130
like JDBC and Hibernate.

00:12:51.130 --> 00:12:53.950
We are integrating with
very popular frameworks

00:12:53.950 --> 00:12:56.320
like Spring Cloud
Integration, for instance.

00:12:56.320 --> 00:13:01.750
And we provide you with tools
for fast, iterative development

00:13:01.750 --> 00:13:02.440
that you can do.

00:13:02.440 --> 00:13:04.240
For instance, the
local emulators

00:13:04.240 --> 00:13:08.650
help you to fast iterate
and develop locally

00:13:08.650 --> 00:13:12.480
without having to set up a
database like Cloud Spanner

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:14.630
or Bigtable database
in the cloud,

00:13:14.630 --> 00:13:17.180
and you have all
this overhead there.

00:13:17.180 --> 00:13:22.880
Also, by moving onto one
of our Cloud databases,

00:13:22.880 --> 00:13:26.150
cloud native databases, you get
all the other features as well.

00:13:26.150 --> 00:13:29.640
So you get high availability,
all the scalability,

00:13:29.640 --> 00:13:33.320
and the integrations that
come with more monitoring

00:13:33.320 --> 00:13:36.480
or optimizing your databases.

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:39.750
Now, you might ask, OK, is
that the only thing that I

00:13:39.750 --> 00:13:43.320
need if I make a decision
to which database to move?

00:13:43.320 --> 00:13:44.160
And it is not.

00:13:44.160 --> 00:13:47.430
You have to differentiate
between two main areas

00:13:47.430 --> 00:13:48.580
that I see here.

00:13:48.580 --> 00:13:50.910
One is, what are the
technical considerations

00:13:50.910 --> 00:13:53.300
that we have touched on
already in this talk?

00:13:53.300 --> 00:13:55.130
And what are the
business considerations?

00:13:55.130 --> 00:13:55.680
Right?

00:13:55.680 --> 00:13:59.670
So a technical consideration,
again, just briefly,

00:13:59.670 --> 00:14:01.815
what are the tools, and
integration, and support

00:14:01.815 --> 00:14:03.330
you get on the technical side?

00:14:03.330 --> 00:14:05.560
What are the communities,
things like that?

00:14:05.560 --> 00:14:08.490
What is the data model
that you have to go into?

00:14:08.490 --> 00:14:11.100
And what are the
consistency requirements

00:14:11.100 --> 00:14:13.307
that you have for
your workloads?

00:14:13.307 --> 00:14:15.390
Now, on the other hand,
the business consideration

00:14:15.390 --> 00:14:18.000
is things like, can
you reuse the skills

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:21.260
that you have already in your
teams, in your companies?

00:14:21.260 --> 00:14:23.490
Is there a certain
SLA that you need?

00:14:23.490 --> 00:14:26.370
Do you need certain
managed functionality?

00:14:26.370 --> 00:14:28.920
What is the security
requirements, compliance,

00:14:28.920 --> 00:14:30.790
certifications,
things like that?

00:14:30.790 --> 00:14:32.610
So there are many
criterias that you

00:14:32.610 --> 00:14:35.280
need and want to
look at when you make

00:14:35.280 --> 00:14:37.450
your choice for a database.

00:14:37.450 --> 00:14:41.670
Now, that said, for all the
cloud native databases that we

00:14:41.670 --> 00:14:44.490
offer, they are all SLA-backed.

00:14:44.490 --> 00:14:47.370
They all come with data
encryption in-flight

00:14:47.370 --> 00:14:49.740
and addressed.

00:14:49.740 --> 00:14:55.940
And they all are built for
scalability and availability.

00:14:55.940 --> 00:14:58.280
So with this, I want
to focus on like, OK,

00:14:58.280 --> 00:15:00.930
what are the databases
[INAUDIBLE] that you can end up

00:15:00.930 --> 00:15:04.300
with, and especially for your
innovation and transformation

00:15:04.300 --> 00:15:05.810
journeys?

00:15:05.810 --> 00:15:07.310
First, Cloud Spanner.

00:15:07.310 --> 00:15:10.210
So why would you
choose Cloud Spanner?

00:15:10.210 --> 00:15:14.560
First, it is a fully managed
ACID and a relational database.

00:15:14.560 --> 00:15:17.650
It comes with a built-in
high availability.

00:15:17.650 --> 00:15:20.350
So data is distributed
and replicated

00:15:20.350 --> 00:15:23.320
across zones and for our
multi-regional setups

00:15:23.320 --> 00:15:24.890
across regions.

00:15:24.890 --> 00:15:28.150
So if one zone goes
down, your database

00:15:28.150 --> 00:15:29.740
will still be available.

00:15:29.740 --> 00:15:32.800
Now, the second thing
is you are provisioning

00:15:32.800 --> 00:15:34.630
the database for what you need.

00:15:34.630 --> 00:15:38.440
Here, you can scale
flexibly, horizontally.

00:15:38.440 --> 00:15:42.510
So you have an online
elastic capacity scaling.

00:15:42.510 --> 00:15:45.820
And that is actually in a matter
of minutes that you can scale.

00:15:45.820 --> 00:15:48.400
So you're just going
to go either use an API

00:15:48.400 --> 00:15:52.540
or our console to scale
your database, depending

00:15:52.540 --> 00:15:56.590
on instance, actually, depending
on the load that you have.

00:15:56.590 --> 00:15:59.320
And a new feature that
we just added recently,

00:15:59.320 --> 00:16:02.770
you can do things like Query
Optimization Version pinning,

00:16:02.770 --> 00:16:07.000
where, if you have a query
optimizer that we have released

00:16:07.000 --> 00:16:10.240
that you like and you want
to test in your version

00:16:10.240 --> 00:16:13.720
before you actually go in
with this for your production

00:16:13.720 --> 00:16:17.080
workloads, you have the
ability to actually choose

00:16:17.080 --> 00:16:19.180
which kind of
optimizations you want

00:16:19.180 --> 00:16:20.960
to have for your workloads.

00:16:20.960 --> 00:16:23.230
Now, we see a lot
of uptake for Cloud

00:16:23.230 --> 00:16:25.600
Spanner in many,
many, many verticals

00:16:25.600 --> 00:16:29.320
and different diverse
workloads, especially things

00:16:29.320 --> 00:16:31.330
where you need the
consistency constraints,

00:16:31.330 --> 00:16:35.780
where you need the asset
capabilities of this database.

00:16:35.780 --> 00:16:38.140
Now, for Cloud
Bigtable, here we are

00:16:38.140 --> 00:16:41.770
looking at a really, really
performant, key-value,

00:16:41.770 --> 00:16:44.020
wide-column database.

00:16:44.020 --> 00:16:47.410
We see a lot of things like
[INAUDIBLE] or anything

00:16:47.410 --> 00:16:49.940
where you need fast lookups.

00:16:49.940 --> 00:16:53.650
But Cloud Bigtable is optimized
for fast [? ingress ?]

00:16:53.650 --> 00:16:57.660
and fast searches
[INAUDIBLE] reads.

00:16:57.660 --> 00:17:00.920
Cloud Bigtable also
has linear scalability.

00:17:00.920 --> 00:17:04.780
So depending on your workloads
and depending on your traffic

00:17:04.780 --> 00:17:08.200
that you have, you can
flexibly scale linearly

00:17:08.200 --> 00:17:10.960
for your workloads, which
is very, very powerful.

00:17:10.960 --> 00:17:14.250
Again, Cloud Bigtable is
a fully managed database.

00:17:14.250 --> 00:17:17.650
It comes with many,
many client libraries

00:17:17.650 --> 00:17:19.630
in idiomatic languages.

00:17:19.630 --> 00:17:23.079
And you have tools integration
like the Key Visualizer

00:17:23.079 --> 00:17:26.170
that helps you
optimize your schemas.

00:17:26.170 --> 00:17:28.000
I also want to
mention here, if you

00:17:28.000 --> 00:17:31.720
are going onto one of our
cloud native databases,

00:17:31.720 --> 00:17:34.120
these get better over time.

00:17:34.120 --> 00:17:36.333
So Cloud Bigtable, for
instance, is a database

00:17:36.333 --> 00:17:37.750
that we have
developed and started

00:17:37.750 --> 00:17:39.760
to develop 15 years ago.

00:17:39.760 --> 00:17:43.570
It has made many, many
iterations and optimizations

00:17:43.570 --> 00:17:44.630
along the way.

00:17:44.630 --> 00:17:47.530
Now, if you are betting on one
of the cloud native databases,

00:17:47.530 --> 00:17:51.790
we are constantly optimizing
the hardware, the network,

00:17:51.790 --> 00:17:53.550
the functionality
under the hood.

00:17:53.550 --> 00:17:55.660
And you just get
that out of the box.

00:17:55.660 --> 00:17:58.390
You don't have to self-manage,
for instance, the operating

00:17:58.390 --> 00:17:58.900
system.

00:17:58.900 --> 00:18:02.980
You don't have to run
patches or things like that,

00:18:02.980 --> 00:18:05.620
and updates for your
self-managed workloads.

00:18:05.620 --> 00:18:08.920
Here, everything is
fully managed for you.

00:18:08.920 --> 00:18:14.710
Now Firestore is the database
that currently has the highest

00:18:14.710 --> 00:18:16.610
net promoter score.

00:18:16.610 --> 00:18:19.400
Firestore is very
easy to start with.

00:18:19.400 --> 00:18:22.180
And again, it's a serverless,
fully managed database,

00:18:22.180 --> 00:18:26.050
where you pay for what you get,
and you pay for what you use.

00:18:26.050 --> 00:18:27.250
That is important here.

00:18:27.250 --> 00:18:29.890
And it comes with
many, many integrations

00:18:29.890 --> 00:18:32.440
for iOS, Android, web clients.

00:18:32.440 --> 00:18:34.780
You find server
client libraries.

00:18:34.780 --> 00:18:37.810
But a feature that I really,
really like from Firestore

00:18:37.810 --> 00:18:42.340
is the real time
synchronization between devices.

00:18:42.340 --> 00:18:46.000
And that can be a mobile
device with Android, iOS,

00:18:46.000 --> 00:18:47.930
or it can be a web application.

00:18:47.930 --> 00:18:49.820
It is very easy to set up.

00:18:49.820 --> 00:18:52.810
You don't have to
write a lot of code.

00:18:52.810 --> 00:18:54.890
It basically comes
out of the box.

00:18:54.890 --> 00:18:56.500
Another feature which
comes out of box

00:18:56.500 --> 00:18:59.170
here is that you
have offline support.

00:18:59.170 --> 00:19:02.140
So even if you have
connection challenges,

00:19:02.140 --> 00:19:05.020
we do all the things
behind the scenes

00:19:05.020 --> 00:19:07.210
to make sure that
your data is cached

00:19:07.210 --> 00:19:12.940
and the app experience is almost
uninterrupted if your people--

00:19:12.940 --> 00:19:16.630
or your users and your
customers go offline.

00:19:16.630 --> 00:19:20.770
We see with Firestore that many,
many companies are actually

00:19:20.770 --> 00:19:24.850
using Firestore to extend and
modernize their application

00:19:24.850 --> 00:19:27.770
in front of legacy setups.

00:19:27.770 --> 00:19:31.300
So for instance, if you want
to add a more natural and more

00:19:31.300 --> 00:19:35.260
modern mobile
application experience,

00:19:35.260 --> 00:19:39.040
Firestore is one of the
number one choices here

00:19:39.040 --> 00:19:44.440
to achieve that with
your existing backends.

00:19:44.440 --> 00:19:48.190
Now, I don't want to say
that you only want to end up

00:19:48.190 --> 00:19:50.890
in our cloud native databases.

00:19:50.890 --> 00:19:53.770
There are many situations
where you might want to end up

00:19:53.770 --> 00:19:56.480
in an open source database.

00:19:56.480 --> 00:19:59.470
Now, with our managed
open source solutions,

00:19:59.470 --> 00:20:01.180
you can do just that.

00:20:01.180 --> 00:20:06.320
And remember, here, it's about
taking away the toil from you.

00:20:06.320 --> 00:20:09.700
With our managed solutions,
we and our partners

00:20:09.700 --> 00:20:13.630
are taking care of things
like operating system patches,

00:20:13.630 --> 00:20:15.700
database patches,
keeping everything

00:20:15.700 --> 00:20:19.810
secure in the background,
and updating the backend

00:20:19.810 --> 00:20:22.130
in the sense of that you get--

00:20:22.130 --> 00:20:25.150
you can choose when you want to
have your new functionalities

00:20:25.150 --> 00:20:27.490
coming in with your
managed solutions.

00:20:27.490 --> 00:20:28.810
But it's much less toil.

00:20:28.810 --> 00:20:31.930
It is integrated with our
monitoring and our logging

00:20:31.930 --> 00:20:37.630
tools, and integrated with the
billing facilities as well.

00:20:37.630 --> 00:20:41.760
Now, you might have wondered--
there wasn't any data analytics

00:20:41.760 --> 00:20:43.450
database so far.

00:20:43.450 --> 00:20:45.760
So I want to actually
bring in BigQuery here,

00:20:45.760 --> 00:20:48.720
which is our serverless,
fully managed,

00:20:48.720 --> 00:20:51.690
petabytes-scale data
warehouse system.

00:20:51.690 --> 00:20:54.320
So with BigQuery,
you can run analytics

00:20:54.320 --> 00:20:58.980
across petabytes of data in
minutes or terabytes of data

00:20:58.980 --> 00:21:00.670
in seconds.

00:21:00.670 --> 00:21:01.970
It's very feature-rich.

00:21:01.970 --> 00:21:05.730
It has a lot of features
like ML integration.

00:21:05.730 --> 00:21:08.610
You have a natural language
query interface now

00:21:08.610 --> 00:21:10.530
that is just released.

00:21:10.530 --> 00:21:12.480
You have an SQL interface.

00:21:12.480 --> 00:21:15.450
So it speaks SQL so
you can, again, reuse

00:21:15.450 --> 00:21:18.610
your skills that you are
already familiar with.

00:21:18.610 --> 00:21:20.120
What's really
powerful with BigQuery

00:21:20.120 --> 00:21:23.410
is the integration
with other systems.

00:21:23.410 --> 00:21:25.660
So BigQuery allows
you to, for instance,

00:21:25.660 --> 00:21:29.580
run federated queries
into Cloud SQL databases.

00:21:29.580 --> 00:21:33.090
By doing this, you don't need to
move your data from Cloud SQL,

00:21:33.090 --> 00:21:34.800
for instance, to BigQuery.

00:21:34.800 --> 00:21:37.500
You can just query your
data directly from BigQuery.

00:21:37.500 --> 00:21:39.240
And we also just
recently announced

00:21:39.240 --> 00:21:42.780
that it's possible to actually
query data in other clouds

00:21:42.780 --> 00:21:45.200
with BigQuery as well.

00:21:45.200 --> 00:21:50.420
So with this, let's get
all this onto one slide.

00:21:50.420 --> 00:21:53.560
So here, you see the full
data management portfolio

00:21:53.560 --> 00:21:58.540
that we are offering ourselves,
as well as with our partners.

00:21:58.540 --> 00:22:01.060
And we don't stop there.

00:22:01.060 --> 00:22:04.320
You can always run
your database of choice

00:22:04.320 --> 00:22:06.670
that, even if it's
not supported by us

00:22:06.670 --> 00:22:09.220
directly or by our
partners, you can always

00:22:09.220 --> 00:22:11.240
run it on our infrastructure.

00:22:11.240 --> 00:22:15.580
So for instance, if you
look at HANA, or IBM Db2,

00:22:15.580 --> 00:22:18.130
or things like that, you
can always run them directly

00:22:18.130 --> 00:22:20.710
on GCE and Kubernetes.

00:22:20.710 --> 00:22:23.740
So there are many
ways, from in-memory,

00:22:23.740 --> 00:22:27.460
non-relational, relational data
warehouses, everything here

00:22:27.460 --> 00:22:31.000
you see that can be accomplished
with our database and data

00:22:31.000 --> 00:22:32.770
management portfolio.

00:22:32.770 --> 00:22:35.560
Now, I want to have a
little different view

00:22:35.560 --> 00:22:39.580
here as my last slide before
I close out this talk.

00:22:39.580 --> 00:22:43.270
So here, you see the
different data model schemas

00:22:43.270 --> 00:22:47.050
that are available and are
supported by our database

00:22:47.050 --> 00:22:48.940
systems and solutions.

00:22:48.940 --> 00:22:51.310
Now you see that many
of those are either

00:22:51.310 --> 00:22:54.130
supported by our fully
managed offerings,

00:22:54.130 --> 00:22:58.150
by our cloud native offerings,
or through our partner

00:22:58.150 --> 00:23:00.010
offerings that we have here.

00:23:00.010 --> 00:23:02.590
And just for you, for
reference and help,

00:23:02.590 --> 00:23:05.170
if you are in the business
of moving from one cloud

00:23:05.170 --> 00:23:10.440
to another, or you want to set
up a multi-cloud environment,

00:23:10.440 --> 00:23:15.470
we have also put the systems
in other cloud providers

00:23:15.470 --> 00:23:17.380
so you have something
that you can match up

00:23:17.380 --> 00:23:20.145
for our database solutions.

00:23:20.145 --> 00:23:21.480
All right.

00:23:21.480 --> 00:23:27.390
So with this, please tune into
the sections and breakout talks

